# ── Secrets — never commit ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.key
*.pem
*.p12
private.key
credentials.json

# ── Python ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
venv/
*.egg-info/

# ── Generated data ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.crawl4ai/
*.log

# ── Call artefacts and audio ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Untracked 2026-08-31. Two reasons, and the second is the one that decided it:
#
#   1. They are OUTPUTS. Every file under data/ is written by a call — the
#      artifact JSON, the WAV, the MP3 Twilio hands back, and the doctors.json
#      / master.json rows the run upserts into. Committing them versions the
#      results of running the program alongside the program.
#   2. They are RECORDED THIRD-PARTY SPEECH. Every transcript is a receptionist
#      who did not agree to be in a repository, named practices included. The
#      audio was already excluded on that ground; the transcripts say the same
#      words and were not.
#
# The offline suite's corpus sweep reads 'data/3 cases jsons' when it is there
# and REPORTS that it did not run when it is not — see the block around
# `corpus sweep NOT run` in test_realtime_protocol.py. Everything that sweep
# proves semantically is also asserted on hand-built calls that travel with
# the repo, so a bare clone loses the regression sweep and nothing else.
#
# `data/*`, NOT `data/`, AND THE STAR IS THE WHOLE FIX. Git will not descend
# into an excluded DIRECTORY, so with `data/` the negations below never ran —
# `git check-ignore -v --no-index data/db/doctors.json` answered `data/` for
# every one of the thirteen files they were supposed to re-admit. They kept
# working only because they were already tracked, which is precisely the trap
# the note below warns about, sprung by the fix written for it. `data/*`
# excludes the CONTENTS instead, so git descends and a negation can be heard.
data/*
*.wav
*.mp3

# NOT EVERYTHING UNDER data/ IS CALL OUTPUT, and a blanket rule with no
# negations would be a trap rather than a policy: git leaves files that are
# ALREADY tracked alone, so these would keep working here and vanish the first
# time anyone removed and re-added one.
#
#   backchannels/  pre-rendered "mm-hm"/"okay" audio, read at runtime by
#                  agents/voice/backchannel.py. Built by
#                  scripts/make_backchannels.py; fixtures, not output.
#   db/            the JSON backend core/db.py reads when no database is
#                  configured — seed data the program starts FROM.
#   voice_samples/ redistributable Piper samples.
#
# The directory negation is enough for the first two: nothing below re-excludes
# their contents. voice_samples needs its own line because `*.wav` above would
# otherwise catch the files inside it, and the last matching rule wins.
!data/backchannels/
!data/db/
!data/voice_samples/
!data/voice_samples/*.wav

# VERIFY AFTER EDITING THIS BLOCK. Every tracked file must be one git does not
# also ignore, or it is living on borrowed time:
#
#   for f in $(git ls-files); do git check-ignore -q --no-index "$f" \
#       && echo "TRAPPED: $f"; done
#
# That loop found data/calls.json (57 legacy call logs, 56 with transcripts)
# still tracked under the data/ policy, and then found the thirteen files this
# block claims to re-admit. It runs in CI.

# Stray editor/patch temp files
*.tmp.*

# Suite output, when a run is redirected to a file in the repo root.
# Caught by the tracked-but-ignored audit above: `git add -A` staged
# 374 KB of console log as source.
test_out.txt
test_err.txt

# ── Piper TTS voice models ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ~345 MB of redistributable model weights, used only by the CLASSIC pipeline
# (USE_REALTIME=false). They were committed in the root commit and every one of
# the 84 commits carried them, which made the repo unpushable: GitHub rejects
# any file over 100 MB and two of these are 115 MB and 109 MB. Purged from
# history 2026-08-19 (320 MB -> 1.9 MB).
#
# They are not source. Piper hosts them, so fetch rather than commit:
#   https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices
# Drop the .onnx and matching .onnx.json into data/voices/. See
# agents/voice/tts_local.py, which looks them up by name there.
data/voices/*.onnx
data/voices/*.onnx.json
